{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"4a7cd0e891c8a955","name":"card_event_pilot_escape","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Poland/events/card_event_pilot_escape","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"PILOT ESCAPE","text":"Develop an air unit of your main nation. It costs 1 less to deploy and operate.","flavorText":"After the collapse of Poland in 1939, thousands of Polish airmen began a determined effort to escape captivity and continue the fight. Many crossed into Romania and Hungary, where they faced internment. Despite the risk, hundreds managed to escape these camps, using false documents, bribery, or local support. Their goal was to flee occupied Europe entirely and reach France. By early 1940, over 1,200 Polish airmen had escaped to France. When France fell in June, they had to escape again. With the help of French and British forces, most Polish pilots crossed the Channel to reach Great-Britain. In total, more than 8,400 Polish air personnel escaped to the UK. Every successful escape was an act of courage and determination, and brought experienced airmen closer to battle. Many of them joined the Royal Air Force and formed units like No. 303 Squadron, which became one of the most performant fighter units during the Battle of Britain.","faction":"Poland","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":2,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"GameplayTags":["ability.develop"],"Name":"card_event_pilot_escape","title":{"Namespace":"card_poland","Key":"card_event_pilot_escape_title","SourceString":"PILOT ESCAPE","LocalizedString":"PILOT ESCAPE"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Poland","kredits":2,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_poland","Key":"card_event_pilot_escape_text","SourceString":"Develop an air unit of your main nation. It costs 1 less to deploy and operate.","LocalizedString":"Develop an air unit of your main nation. It costs 1 less to deploy and operate."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_poland","Key":"card_event_pilot_escape_history","SourceString":"After the collapse of Poland in 1939, thousands of Polish airmen began a determined effort to escape captivity and continue the fight. Many crossed into Romania and Hungary, where they faced internment. Despite the risk, hundreds managed to escape these camps, using false documents, bribery, or local support. Their goal was to flee occupied Europe entirely and reach France. By early 1940, over 1,200 Polish airmen had escaped to France. When France fell in June, they had to escape again. With the help of French and British forces, most Polish pilots crossed the Channel to reach Great-Britain. In total, more than 8,400 Polish air personnel escaped to the UK. Every successful escape was an act of courage and determination, and brought experienced airmen closer to battle. Many of them joined the Royal Air Force and formed units like No. 303 Squadron, which became one of the most performant fighter units during the Battle of Britain.","LocalizedString":"After the collapse of Poland in 1939, thousands of Polish airmen began a determined effort to escape captivity and continue the fight. Many crossed into Romania and Hungary, where they faced internment. Despite the risk, hundreds managed to escape these camps, using false documents, bribery, or local support. Their goal was to flee occupied Europe entirely and reach France. By early 1940, over 1,200 Polish airmen had escaped to France. When France fell in June, they had to escape again. With the help of French and British forces, most Polish pilots crossed the Channel to reach Great-Britain. In total, more than 8,400 Polish air personnel escaped to the UK. Every successful escape was an act of courage and determination, and brought experienced airmen closer to battle. Many of them joined the Royal Air Force and formed units like No. 303 Squadron, which became one of the most performant fighter units during the Battle of Britain."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommo